with so much of  information available out there  including watching this video  i'm sure you've come across lots of  different advice  add in the fact that there are so many  fitness goals and  so many different ways to achieve those  goals it can get  really confusing on what works and what  is  just too good to be true so let's talk  about the seven common fitness  misconceptions  and separate facts from myth  let's get the facts right you can't turn  fat  into muscle there are two completely  different tissues  it's like saying i want to turn an apple  into a banana  it just doesn't work that way adipose of  fatty tissue is found  under the skin sandwiched between  muscles  and around the internal organs like the  heart  muscle tissue is found throughout the  body  what weight training does is that it  helps to build muscle  which encourages fat loss by increasing  your resting metabolism  one pound of muscle burns 7 to 13  calories  whereas one pound of fat only burns 2 to  3 calories  so if you have more lean muscles your  body will burn more calories  throughout the day hence looking slimmer  and leaner  i've also come across this question too  many times  can i work out and not diet basically  just eat whatever i want  how i wish this was true because i love  my food too  but unfortunately it's not i'm sure you  have heard of this saying  you are what you eat nutrition and diet  is  80 of the results we achieve even if you  spend  hours sweating and killing yourself in  the gym  but if you eat as much as you want after  then  you're just wasting all your effort and  you won't get the results you desire  say if you were to burn 500 calories  working out  but you were to consume a huge meal with  dessert  racking up to 1200 calories  then you're just undoing all your effort  to manipulate your weight up or down  it comes down to one simple principle of  energy balance  to lose weight you have to create a  negative energy balance  that means your total daily energy  expenditure  should be more than what you consume  that can be achieved by increasing your  activity level  and reducing your food portion  some people think they need to spend  hours of cardio in order to lose the  excess pounds  but they refuse to lift weights in fear  of becoming  bulky yes doing cardio is great for your  heart health  but it is not the most efficient way to  melt off  the fat and lean up i personally  rarely perform steady paced cardio as  i'm able to maintain my physique through  a combination of strength training  and heat exercises as i've mentioned in  my first point  strength training builds muscles and  muscles  burns more calories studies have also  shown that women who regularly engage in  strength training activity  will burn nearly 50 more calories  than their cardio queen counterparts  that translates  about an extra 100 calories expanded per  day  even when your body is at rest and  ladies  no you will not bulk up we have 15 to 20  times  lower testosterone levels than men  therefore becoming bulky just by adding  weights to our fitness routine  is highly unlikely instead adding weight  training will make  us stronger and shape our body on the  other hand  hit workouts are a great way to burn  more calories in a short period of time  you will also benefit from the after  burn the calories  you keep burning even after your workout  is done  it also helps to improve performance  such as explosive strength  agility and endurance saying that  however  hit workouts are not for everybody it's  not suitable for beginners or those who  have a history of injuries  and can even lead to overtraining hence  i would only recommend to perform  heat workouts two times per week  once again this goes back to the first  point fat cannot turn into muscles  and muscles cannot turn into fat there  are two completely different tissues  i think this myth came about because we  tend to see our super fit  sporting heroes becoming fat and out of  shape  once they stop training so what's really  happening  when you stop training you will lose  muscle mass  hence feeling less firm or toned  if you continue to remain inactive but  continue to consume the same amount of  calories  as previously then your body will start  to put on fat  because you are consuming more than what  your body is able to burn  with a decrease in muscle mass your  calories should also be decreased  as the caloric demand is no longer as  high as it previously was  when you were active if your calories  are not lowered  you will begin to slowly gain fat and  that is where the confusion  lies there is  no such thing as spot reduction you've  probably heard me saying this  over and over again yet  i keep receiving comments stating that i  only want to lose my hips  and belly fat even worse there are  workouts out there claiming that you can  get a flat belly in two weeks  or even a collarbone workout like  for real toning one area won't  just burn fat from that area and reveal  a slimmer sexier abs  when we lose weight we lose fat from our  entire body  you need to train the whole body and  keep your nutrition in check  covering the abdominals is a layer of  fat  hence healthy eating is the key to  minimizing fat  stores around the midsection depending  on your body shape  there will be areas of your body where  fat loss happens quicker compared to  other parts  these areas are just the stubborn areas  that  hold more fat which means further fat  loss  is required to see results in these  areas  of course by adding strength training  you can add  more muscle definition to your body but  that definition will only be visible if  you have a lower body fat percentage  remember the principle of weight loss  it's all about  energy balance whatever your goal is  whether to increase performance  or to pack on muscle you need good cups  and plenty of them i love my cups  and it has helped me to reduce my body  fat and lean up  cubs aren't the enemy a sedentary life  high sugar diet over dieting over  eating or having the wrong training  program are  if you were to follow a restricted diet  and were to eat your normal diet again  the weight will come back on and even  more  i've always been a believer of balance  and moderation  make healthier food choices consume a  balanced diet filled with whole foods  and take everything in moderation  i guess it's common for us to have the  more  the better mindset in fact i've known of  trainers who trained their clients  six to eight hours a day in order to  produce  fast weight loss results for a fitness  competition yes  they did lose a lot of weight within the  time frame of the competition  however what you have to ask yourself is  is this lifestyle sustainable and what  will it do to their long-term  mental and physical health the people i  know have gained  more weight after the show and it has  impacted their self-esteem  the truth is they can't keep up with  their workout and sustain it for life  over training can also result in extreme  fatigue  low energy mood swings hormone imbalance  injuries and a decline in performance  it's gonna do  more damage than good having enough time  for your muscles to recover is extremely  important  for you to perform at your optimum and  to see permanent results  a solid workout should last between 45  minutes to an hour  if you find yourself spending longer  than that then you should really review  your training  are you spending more time training or  taking long breaks  on your phone or catching up with your  workout buddy  this list could go on and i'm sure you  might have come across  other fitness misconceptions or perhaps  you might have questions  which you're unsure of let me know in  the comments below  the bottom line is there isn't one  method fits all  the more you learn about your own body  through nutrition and training  and the more you educate yourself in all  areas of fitness  the better you will be in creating and  implementing  these informations and techniques that  will help you achieve  your fitness goals if you enjoyed this  video then give this video a thumbs up  share this video on and be sure to hit  the subscribe button  check out my youtube channel for a lot  more fitness tips nutritional tips  healthy recipes and workout videos and i  will see you in my next video  all the best

